  And the vines genuinely cross: two diagram points coincide at the
  isosceles instants, which is what a crossing IS. Minimum separation
  over one T/3 loop, sampled finely:
    min 5.58e-09, max 2.63e-03

============================================================================
O3. Is the isosceles identification exact or approximate?
============================================================================
  At an isosceles configuration with apex k, the whole configuration is
  symmetric under reflection in the perpendicular bisector, and that
  reflection is an isometry of the DENSITY that swaps the other two
  bodies. Their maxima are therefore exchanged by an isometry, so their
  birth values are exactly equal, whatever the kernel and however far the
  maxima are displaced from the bodies. Constructed exactly:

     apex at   sigma    |birth_i - birth_j|   max displacement from body
      body 3    0.30              0.000e+00                       0.0000
      body 3    0.75              0.000e+00                       0.1575
      body 3    0.30              0.000e+00                       0.0001
      body 3    0.75  (degenerate at this sigma)
      body 3    0.30              0.000e+00                       0.0000
      body 3    0.75              2.220e-16                       0.2977
      body 3    0.30              0.000e+00                       0.0000
      body 3    0.75  (degenerate at this sigma)

  Exactly zero, at displacements of order 0.1. The identification is a
  symmetry argument, not a first-order approximation.

============================================================================
O4. Is the diagram exactly invariant under rigid motion?
============================================================================
  200 random rotations and translations: worst change in the diagram 4.44e-16
  rho(R^-1 x - a) is rho composed with an isometry, so the critical VALUES
  are identical, not nearly identical. Exact by construction.

